{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:MassTheory.AlphaKDerivationProcedure","@type":"LeanModule","name":"MassTheory.AlphaKDerivationProcedure","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Fintype.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Matrix.Mul","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.NonsingularInverse"],"declarations":1,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.alpha_k_derivation_procedure","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.alpha_k_derivation_procedure","kind":"theorem","line":42,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/alpha-k-derivation-procedure.lean#L42-L69","doc":"**Proposition (linear-system characterisation of `y_λ`)**.\n\nAt generic `q` the character matrix `A = [χ_λ(w_μ)]` is invertible\n(Schur–Weyl–Hecke duality), so the Jones–Markov linear system\n`A *ᵥ y = b` (with `b_μ = z^{n - len μ}`) has a **unique** solution\n`y`.  This is the abstract content: for an invertible square matrix\n`A` over `ℝ` and any `b`, `A *ᵥ y = b` has a unique solution,\nnamely `y = A⁻¹ *ᵥ b`. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:alpha-k-derivation-procedure","label":"prop:alpha-k-derivation-procedure","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/alpha-k-derivation-procedure.md"}],"signatureChunk":0}]}